explanation:
This complete component example can be rendered by the React lesson runner:
```react
function Profile(props) {
  return <section>
    <h2>{props.name}</h2>
    <p>Learning {props.subject}</p>
  </section>;
}
function App() {
  return <main>
    <Profile name="Tara" subject="Art" />
    <Profile name="Luis" subject="Music" />
  </main>;
}
```
You see two profiles: Tara learning Art, and Luis learning Music. Props are inputs passed from a parent component to a child. Profile reads those inputs; it should not edit them.

Here is a separate complete interactive App. Replace the earlier App with this one to try it:
```react
function App() {
  const [taps, setTaps] = React.useState(0);
  return <main>
    <p>Taps: {taps}</p>
    <button onClick={() => setTaps(current => current + 1)}>
      Tap once
    </button>
  </main>;
}
```
Initially you see Taps: 0. One click shows 1, then another shows 2. useState returns two values: the current state and a setter. Square brackets unpack that pair into taps and setTaps.

