explanation:
Run this complete App in the React/JSX lesson runner:
```react
function App() {
  const [draft, setDraft] = React.useState('');
  return <main>
    <label>New note
      <input value={draft}
        onChange={event => setDraft(event.target.value)} />
    </label>
    <p>You typed: {draft}</p>
  </main>;
}
```
Typing hello updates both the field and paragraph. event is the change information; event.target is the input involved; .value is its current text. The arrow receives that event when editing happens.

This is a separate complete App; replace the first App to try it:
```react
function App() {
  const [draft, setDraft] = React.useState('');
  const [notes, setNotes] = React.useState([]);
  const [message, setMessage] = React.useState('');
  function submit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    const cleaned = draft.trim();
    if (cleaned.length === 0) {
      setMessage('Write a note first.');
      return;
    }
    setNotes(current => [...current, cleaned]);
    setDraft('');
    setMessage('Note added.');
  }
  return <main>
    <form onSubmit={submit}>
      <label>New note <input value={draft}
        onChange={e => setDraft(e.target.value)} /></label>
      <button type="submit">Add note</button>
    </form>
    <p role="status">{message}</p>
    <ul>{notes.map((note, i) => <li key={i}>{note}</li>)}</ul>
  </main>;
}
```
Submitting spaces shows Write a note first. and no item. Submitting '  Sketch  ' adds one Sketch item, clears the field, and shows Note added.

