The supplied reversal uses [...current].reverse(). Spread creates a new array container with the same person objects. reverse() reverses that new array in place and returns it; calling current.reverse() instead would mutate the state array. The setter’s updater receives pending state and returns the copied order. This copy is shallow; the person objects are not changed here.

A small ordinary JavaScript example isolates the array operation, without another component:
```javascript
