explanation:
A Suspense boundary surrounds children that can suspend while waiting. fallback is replacement UI, not an error handler. Here is a complete App using a controlled local Promise, with no downloaded module:
```react
let finishGreeting;
const greetingModule = new Promise(resolve => {
  finishGreeting = () => resolve({default: Greeting});
});
function Greeting() { return <p data-greeting>Hello, explorer</p>; }
const LazyGreeting = React.lazy(() => greetingModule);
function App() {
  return <main><button onClick={() => finishGreeting()}>Finish local load</button>
    <React.Suspense fallback={<p role="status">Preparing greeting</p>}>
      <LazyGreeting />
    </React.Suspense>
  </main>;
}
```
The frame mounts App. React first tries LazyGreeting, invokes its loader and receives the pending greetingModule Promise. Preparing greeting appears until the button calls finishGreeting(). That resolves an object whose default is the Greeting component, allowing React to show Hello, explorer. Greeting’s code was already in this file.

This changed version resolves the same local Promise before the frame mounts App:
```react
let finishGreeting;
const greetingModule = new Promise(resolve => {
  finishGreeting = () => resolve({default: Greeting});
});
function Greeting() { return <p data-greeting>Hello, explorer</p>; }
const LazyGreeting = React.lazy(() => greetingModule);
finishGreeting();
function App() {
  return <main><button onClick={() => finishGreeting()}>Finish local load</button>
    <React.Suspense fallback={<p role="status">Preparing greeting</p>}>
      <LazyGreeting />
    </React.Suspense>
  </main>;
}
```
The greeting becomes available without clicking. Do not promise an observable loading frame for an already-settled Promise; the check waits for the greeting instead.

This exercise controls a Promise manually so you can inspect waiting without relying on network timing. new Promise(resolve => { ... }) immediately provides a resolve function; finishLoading saves a callback that resolves it with { default: Report }. The Report code is already present: this simulates a pending module and does not split or download a real file.

This smaller Promise example exposes the callback and its effect:
```javascript
let finish;
const pending = new Promise(resolve => { finish = resolve; });
pending.then(value => console.log(value));
finish("Ready");
```
The constructor takes an executor callback and returns a pending Promise. It immediately gives the callback a resolve function, stored here as finish. Calling finish supplies the eventual result, so the reaction later prints Ready. No network request or downloaded module is involved.

